when the strawberries are ripe it's  seasonal workers who come to these  fields in Belgium to pick them though  the conditions here are rosy elsewhere  in Europe seasonal workers from non-eu  countries often get a raw deal they  often housed in temporary shelters made  of plastic and cardboard the EU is  working on legislation to put an end to  the precarious conditions you may  remember the terrible situation in  Rosano and Italy there were migrant  workers were suffering very badly and  that was the impetus for this the new  rules voted on by M EPS include a  minimum wage to stop undercutting pay  and proper accommodation as well as a  maximum duration of stay if it passes a  parliamentary vote in twenty fourteen  countries would have two years to comply  however the UK would be an exception we  don't opt in normally on the areas of  immigration migration immigration so but  that isn't say we don't have a view at  least I have a view very strongly on the  sort of conditions in which seasonal  workers have to exist and have to work  and that's why I have supported the the  measures today some polish em EPS were  concerned about how the new rules will  affect their agreements with third  countries like Ukraine and Belarus many  colleagues in Eastern Europe may have  concerns that it's my job to switch  those concerns it's not about the volume  of migrants coming in it's about basic  conditions and that's the important  thing so for any colleagues who have  concerns they shouldn't have concerns  it's about ensuring that you don't have  extremes of exploitation EU countries  would have to carry out inspections and  employers breaking the rules would face  stiff penalties in the aim of bringing  an end to the cycle of exploitation
 

